#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:60
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduccion"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:62
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&lisa; is intended to provide a kind of <quote>network neighborhood</quote>, "
"but only relying on the TCP/IP protocol stack, no smb or whatever."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:66
#, no-c-format
msgid "It is completely independent from &kde;/&Qt;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:68
#, no-c-format
msgid "The list of running hosts is provided via <acronym>TCP</acronym> port 7741."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:71
#, no-c-format
msgid "&lisa; supports two ways of finding hosts:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:75
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You give &lisa; a range of <acronym>IP</acronym> addresses, then &lisa; will "
"send <acronym>ICMP</acronym> echo requests to all given <acronym>IP</"
"acronym> addresses, and wait for the answers."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:80
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can tell &lisa; to execute <command>nmblookup</command> <option>\"*</"
"option>. The command line tool <command>nmblookup</command> must be "
"installed from the Samba package. <command>nmblookup</command> <option>\"*"
"\"</option> sends a broadcast to the attached networks, and all hosts "
"running <acronym>SMB</acronym> services will answer this broadcast."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:93
#, no-c-format
msgid "How it works"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:95
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the configuration file you provide a range of IP-addresses which &lisa; "
"should check to see whether they are running."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:98
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the most simple case this could be your network address/subnetmask, then "
"&lisa; would check every possible host of your network to see if it is "
"running."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:102
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The hosts are checked using <acronym>ICMP</acronym> echo requests. To be "
"able to send and receive <acronym>ICMP</acronym> echo requests and replies "
"the program has to open a so-called <quote>raw socket</quote>. Therefore it "
"needs <systemitem class=\"username\">root</systemitem> privileges. This "
"socket is opened right after the start of the program, after successfully "
"opening the socket root privileges are dropped immediately (see "
"<filename>main.cpp</filename> and <filename>strictmain.cpp</filename>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:112
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you configure &lisa; so that it also uses <command>nmblookup</command>, "
"it will <literal>popen(\"nmblookup \\\"*\\\"\")</literal> and then parse the "
"results."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:116
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Since the <acronym>ICMP</acronym> requests and the broadcasts can cause some "
"network traffic if there are more than one such server running in one "
"network, the servers cooperate with each other. Before they start pinging "
"(or <command>nmblookup</command>), they send a broadcast on port 7741."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:122
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If somebody answers this broadcast, they will retrieve the complete list of "
"running hosts via <acronym>TCP</acronym> port 7741 from this host and will "
"not start to ping (or <command>nmblookup</command>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:127
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If nobody answers, the host which sent the broadcast will start pinging the "
"hosts (or <command>nmblookup</command>) and then open a socket which listens "
"for the mentioned broadcasts. If the host received an answer to his "
"broadcast, it won't have the socket for listening to the broadcasts open. So "
"usually exactly one of the servers will have this socket open and only this "
"one will actually ping (or <command>nmblookup</command>) the hosts."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:135
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In other words, the servers are lazy, they work like <quote>I will only do "
"something if nobody else can do it for me</quote>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:138
#, no-c-format
msgid "There is another feature which reduces the network load."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:140
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Let's say you configured &lisa; to update every 10 minutes. Now you don't "
"access your server very often. If nobody accesses the server for the last "
"update period, the server will update (either itself or from the one which "
"actually does the work) and then double its update period, &ie; the next "
"update will happen after 20 minutes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:146
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This will happen 4 times, so if nobody accesses the server with update "
"period 10 minutes for a long time, its update interval will increase up to "
"160 minutes, almost three hours. If then somebody accesses the data from the "
"server, he will get an old list ( up to 160 minutes old). With accessing the "
"server will reset its update interval to its initial value, &ie; 10 minutes "
"and immediately start updating if the last update is more than these 10 "
"minutes over. This means if you get a very old list, you can try some "
"seconds later again and you should get a current version."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:156
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This will have fast effect for the servers, which don't ping (or nmblookup) "
"theirselves, since only one user usually accesses them, and it will have "
"less effect for the server which does the pinging (or <command>nmblookup</"
"command>), since this server is accessed from all other servers in the "
"network."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:162
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This way it is possible that many hosts in a network run this server, but "
"the net load will remain low. For the user it is not necessary to know "
"wether there is a server (&ie; a name server or fileserver or whatever) in "
"the network which also runs &lisa;. He can always run &lisa; locally and "
"&lisa; will detect if there is one, transparently to the user."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:169
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The first client for &lisa; is an ioslave for &kde; 2, so the user can enter "
"there <userinput>lan://localhost/</userinput> or <userinput>lan:/</"
"userinput>, which will both contact &lisa; on the own system."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:174
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If there is a machine which runs all the time and the user knows that this "
"machine also runs &lisa;, he can use his &lisa; client directly with this "
"server (would be with the mentioned ioslave <userinput>lan://the_server_name/"
"</userinput>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:179
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you don't want that your &lisa; takes part in the broadcasting, but "
"always does the pinging itself, make it use another port with the command "
"line option <option>--port</option> or <option>-p</option>. This is not "
"recommended!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:184
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you send <command>SIGHUP</command> to &lisa;, it will reread its "
"configfile. If you send <command>SIGUSR1</command> to &lisa;, it will print "
"some status information to stdout."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:188
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The data provided over the socket has a simple format: <computeroutput>&lt;"
"decimal ip address in network byte order&gt;&lt;one space 0x20&gt;&lt;full "
"name of the host&gt;&lt;a terminating '\\0'&gt;&lt;newline '\\n'&lt;</"
"computeroutput> and the last line <computeroutput>0 succeeded&lt;'\\n'&gt;</"
"computeroutput>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:197
#, no-c-format
msgid "For example,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:199
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<computeroutput>17302538 some_host.whatever.de\n"
"18285834 linux.whatever.de\n"
"17827082 nameserver.whatever.de\n"
"0 succeeded</computeroutput>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:201
#, no-c-format
msgid "This should make it easy parseable."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:203
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If there are very strict security rules in your network, some people might "
"consider the pinging a potential attack. If you have problems with this, try "
"the restricted version, &reslisa;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:210
#, no-c-format
msgid "&reslisa;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:212
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you hav very strict security rules in your network or you don't want to "
"have another port open or whatever, you can use &reslisa;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:216
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With &reslisa; you cannot ping whole networks and address ranges, you can "
"give &reslisa; up to currently 64 hosts by their names in its config file. "
"These will be pinged. You are still able to use <command>nmblookup</command>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:221
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&reslisa; will also only provide the information over a unix domain socket, "
"&ie; not over the network. The name of the socket is <filename>/tmp/resLisa-"
"YourLoginname</filename> so &reslisa; can be safely run by more users on one "
"machine."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:226
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Since it should also not produce a security risk of any kind it is safe to "
"install &reslisa; setuid <systemitem class=\"username\">root</systemitem>. "
"<systemitem class=\"username\">root</systemitem> privileges will be dropped "
"right after startup (see <filename>strictmain.cpp</filename>), they are only "
"needed to create a raw socket for sending the <acronym>ICMP</acronym> echo "
"requests."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:234
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It will also not send or receive broadcasts. The first client for this is "
"also an ioslave for &kde; 2 (<userinput>rlan:/</userinput> in &konqueror; "
"for example.)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:241
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Configuration File"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:243
#, no-c-format
msgid "Now an example config file:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:245
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"PingAddresses = 192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0;192.168.100.10-"
"192.168.199.19;192.168.200.1;192-192.168-168.100-199.0-9;\n"
"PingNames = bb_mail;\n"
"AllowedAddresses = 192.168.0.0/255.255.0.0\n"
"BroadcastNetwork = 192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0\n"
"SearchUsingNmblookup = 1                #also try nmblookup\n"
"FirstWait = 30                          #30 hundredth seconds\n"
"SecondWait = -1                         #only one try\n"
"#SecondWait = 60                         #try twice, and the second time "
"wait 0.6 seconds\n"
"UpdatePeriod = 300                      #update after 300 secs\n"
"DeliverUnnamedHosts = 0                 #don't publish hosts without name\n"
"MaxPingsAtOnce = 256                    #send up to 256 ICMP echo requests "
"at once"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: option
#: index.docbook:248
#, no-c-format
msgid "PingAddresses"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:250
#, no-c-format
msgid "This is probably the most important entry."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:252
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Here you say which addresses will be pinged. You can specify multiple "
"ranges, they are divided by semicolons."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:255
#, no-c-format
msgid "There are four possible ways to define addresses:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:259
#, no-c-format
msgid "net address/network mask"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:261
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0, &ie; an <acronym>IP</acronym> address and the "
"assigned network mask."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:264
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This doesn't have to be the network address and netmask of your machine. For "
"example, if you have 10.0.0.0/255.0.0.0 as your own address, you could "
"specify 10.1.2.0/255.255.255.0 if you are only interested in these "
"addresses. The combination <acronym>IP</acronym> address-network mask must "
"be divided by a slash <quote>/</quote> and the address does not have to be a "
"real network address, it can also be a host address of the desired network, "
"&ie; 10.12.34.67/255.0.0.0 is the same as 10.0.0.0/255.0.0.0 ."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:276
#, no-c-format
msgid "a range of <acronym>IP</acronym> addresses"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:278
#, no-c-format
msgid "For example: 192.168.100.10-192.168.199.19"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:280
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"An <acronym>IP</acronym>-address where pinging will start and an "
"<acronym>IP</acronym>-address where pinging will end."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:283
#, no-c-format
msgid "Both addresses must be divided by a <quote>-</quote>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:285
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In this example this would produce 199-100+1=100, 100*256=25.600, 25.600+(19-"
"10+1)=25.590 addresses"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:291
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"An <acronym>IP</acronym> address, as represented by ranges of each of the "
"four decimal numbers"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:294
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"An <acronym>IP</acronym> address can be represented by its four decimal "
"numbers, and you can specify ranges four each of these four numbers: 192-"
"192.169-171.100-199.0-9"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:299
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In this example all <acronym>IP</acronym> addresses with first number 192, "
"second number from 168 to 168, third number from 100 up to 199 and last "
"number from 0 up to 9 will be pinged. This would give 1*1*100*10=1.000 "
"addresses."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:304
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is probably only useful in very seldom cases. Here you have to provide "
"ranges for every four numbers, always divided by <quote>-</quote>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:311
#, no-c-format
msgid "Single <acronym>IP</acronym> addresses or host names"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:313
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <acronym>IP</acronym> address or host name of any machine you are "
"particularly interested in."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:319 index.docbook:329
#, no-c-format
msgid "It is also valid to leave this entry empty."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: option
#: index.docbook:324
#, no-c-format
msgid "PingNames"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:326
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Here you can additionally specify hosts to ping using their names. The names "
"have to be divided by semicolons."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: option
#: index.docbook:334
#, no-c-format
msgid "AllowedAddresses"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:336
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is also very important. &lisa; will only ping addresses, accept clients "
"and answer broadcasts from addresses, which are covered by the addresses "
"given in this line. You can add up to 32 network addresses/network masks or "
"single addresses. Divide them by ; and don't put empty space between the "
"addresses!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:342
#, no-c-format
msgid "For example, 192.168.0.0/255.255.0.0;192.169.0.0"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:344
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A complete network and a single address are valid. Always make this as "
"strict as possible, usually your network address/subnetmask is a good choice."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: option
#: index.docbook:351
#, no-c-format
msgid "BroadcastNetwork"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:353
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This entry contains exactly one network address/subnet mask. To this network "
"broadcasts will be sent. Usually this should be your own network address/"
"subnetmask, for example: 192.168.0.0/255.255.0.0"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: option
#: index.docbook:360
#, no-c-format
msgid "SearchUsingNmblookup"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:362
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Here you can give <parameter>0</parameter> or <parameter>1</parameter>. "
"<parameter>1</parameter> means that &lisa; will execute <command>nmblookup</"
"command> <option>\"*\"</option> and parse the output from this command. This "
"produces less network traffic than the pinging, but you will only get hosts "
"which have a <acronym>SMB</acronym> service running (&Windows; machines or "
"machines running samba)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:370
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you enable this option and also give <acronym>IP</acronym> addresses to "
"ping, then <command>nmblookup</command> will be executed first and then the "
"pinging will start. Then only addresses will be pinged, which were not "
"already delivered from <command>nmblookup</command>. This should slightly "
"decrease the network load."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: option
#: index.docbook:380
#, no-c-format
msgid "FirstWait"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:382
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If &lisa; pings, &ie; if it sends the <acronym>ICMP</acronym> echo requests, "
"it sends a bunch of requests at once, and the it will wait for the number of "
"hundredth seconds you specify here. Usually values from 5 to 50 should be "
"good, the maximum is 99 (gives 0.99 seconds, a very long time). Try to make "
"this value as small as possible while still finding all running hosts."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: option
#: index.docbook:392
#, no-c-format
msgid "SecondWait"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:394
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"After &lisa; has sent the echo requests the first time, it can be possible "
"that some hosts were not found. To improve the results, &lisa; can ping a "
"second time. This time it will only ping hosts, from which it didn't receive "
"answers. If you have good results with pinging only once, you can disable "
"the second time with setting SecondWait to <userinput>-1</userinput>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:401
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Otherwise it might be a good idea to make this value a little bit bigger "
"than the value for <option>FirstWait</option>, since the hosts which were "
"not found on the first try, are probably slower or further away so they "
"might take some milliseconds longer to answer. Usually values from 5 to 50 "
"should be good or -1 to disable the second scan. The maximum is 99 (gives "
"0.99 seconds, a very long time)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: option
#: index.docbook:411
#, no-c-format
msgid "UpdatePeriod"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:413
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is the interval after which &lisa; will update. After this time &lisa; "
"will again ping or <command>nmblookup</command> or get the list of hosts "
"from the &lisa; server which actually does the pinging."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:418
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Valid values are between 30 seconds and 1800 seconds (half an hour). If you "
"have a big network, don't make the interval too small (to keep network load "
"low). Values from 300 to 900 seconds (5 to 15 minutes) might be a good idea."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:423
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Keep in mind that the update period is doubled if nobody accesses the "
"server, up to 4 times, so the interval will become 16 times the value given "
"here and will be reseted to the value given here if somebody accesses the "
"server."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: option
#: index.docbook:431
#, no-c-format
msgid "DeliverUnnamedHosts"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:433
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If an answer to an echo request from an IP address was received, were &lisa; "
"could not determine a name, it will be only delivered over the port if you "
"set this to 1."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:437
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"I am not really sure if this is a useful feature, but maybe there are some "
"infrastructure devices in your network without assigned names, so they don't "
"have to be published. Set this to 0 if you want to keep them secret ;-) If "
"unsure, say 0."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:445
#, no-c-format
msgid "MaxPingsAtOnce"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:447
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When sending the pings (echo requests), &lisa; sends a bunch of these at "
"once and then waits for the answers. By default there are 256 pings sent at "
"once, usually you should not need the change this value. If you make it much "
"bigger, the internal receive buffers for the answers to the echo requests "
"may become to small, if you make it to small, the updating will be slower."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:457
#, no-c-format
msgid "Three more example files"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:460
#, no-c-format
msgid "FIXME"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:462
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You are member of a small network with 24 bit network mask, &ie; up to 256 "
"hosts:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:465
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"PingAddresses = 192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0\n"
"AllowedAddresses = 192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0\n"
"BroadcastNetwork = 192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0\n"
"SearchUsingNmblookup = 0                         #don't use nmblookup\n"
"FirstWait = 20                                   #20 hundredth seconds\n"
"SecondWait = 30                                  #30 hundredth seconds on "
"the seconds try\n"
"UpdatePeriod = 300                               #update after 300 secs\n"
"DeliverUnnamedHosts = 0                          #don't publish hosts "
"without name"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:470
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration file for hosts running <acronym>SMB</acronym> only"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:473
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You are only interested in hosts running <acronym>SMB</acronym> services and "
"you don't have routers in your network:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:476
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"AllowedAddresses = 192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0\n"
"BroadcastNetwork = 192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0\n"
"SearchUsingNmblookup = 1                #use nmblookup\n"
"UpdatePeriod = 300                      #update after 300 secs\n"
"DeliverUnnamedHosts = 0                 #don't publish hosts without name"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:480
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration file using both <command>nmblookup</command> and pinging"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:483
#, no-c-format
msgid "The same network, but here both nmblookup and pinging is used."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:486
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"PingAddresses = 192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0\n"
"PingNames = bb_mail\n"
"AllowedAddresses = 192.168.0.0/255.255.0.0\n"
"BroadcastNetwork = 192.168.100.0/255.255.255.0\n"
"SearchUsingNmblookup = 1                #also try nmblookup\n"
"FirstWait = 30                          #30 hundredth seconds\n"
"SecondWait = -1                         #only one try\n"
"#SecondWait = 60                         #try twice, and the second time "
"wait 0.6 seconds\n"
"UpdatePeriod = 300                      #update after 300 secs\n"
"DeliverUnnamedHosts = 0                 #don't publish hosts without name\n"
"MaxPingsAtOnce = 256                    #send up to 256 ICMP echo requests "
"at once"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:491
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration file for &reslisa;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:493
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"And now a configuration file for &reslisa;, PingAddresses is not used by "
"&reslisa;, neither is BroadcastNetwork."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:496
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"PingNames = bb_mail;some_host;some_other_host\n"
"AllowedAddresses = 192.168.0.0/255.255.0.0\n"
"SearchUsingNmblookup = 1                # use nmblookup\n"
"FirstWait = 30                          #30 hundredth seconds\n"
"SecondWait = -1                         #only one try\n"
"#SecondWait = 60                         #try twice, and the second time "
"wait 0.6 seconds\n"
"UpdatePeriod = 300                      #update after 300 secs\n"
"DeliverUnnamedHosts = 1                 #also publish hosts without name\n"
"MaxPingsAtOnce = 256                    #send up to 256 ICMP echo requests "
"at once"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:503
#, no-c-format
msgid "Command Line Options and Other Usage"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:505
#, no-c-format
msgid "The following command line options are supported:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:509
#, no-c-format
msgid "<option>-v</option>, <option>--version</option>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:511
#, no-c-format
msgid "Prints brief version information."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:516
#, no-c-format
msgid "<option>-h</option>, <option>--help</option>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:518
#, no-c-format
msgid "Gives an overview of the command line options"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:523
#, no-c-format
msgid "<option>-u</option>, <option>--unix</option>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:525
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Search at first for <filename>$<envar>HOME</envar>/.lisarc</filename>, then "
"for <filename>/etc/lisarc</filename>. This is the default behavior."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:532
#, no-c-format
msgid "<option>-k</option>, <option>--kde1</option>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:534
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Search first for <filename>$<envar>HOME</envar>/.kde/share/config/lisarc</"
"filename>, then for <filename>$<envar>KDEDIR</envar>/share/config/lisarc</"
"filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:542
#, no-c-format
msgid "<option>-K</option>, <option>--kde2</option>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:544
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Looks for the file <filename>lisarc</filename> in every folder returned by "
"running <userinput><command>kde-config</command> <option>--path</option> "
"<parameter>config</parameter></userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:551
#, no-c-format
msgid "<option>-c</option>, <option>--config=</option><parameter>FILE</parameter>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:554
#, no-c-format
msgid "Read <parameter>FILE</parameter> and no other configuration file."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:560
#, no-c-format
msgid "<option>-p</option>, <option>--port</option> <parameter>PORTNR</parameter>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:563
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Start the server on this portnumber. If you use this, &lisa; won't be able "
"to cooperate with other &lisa;'s on the network. This option is not "
"available for &reslisa;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:571
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you send the Hangup-Signal to &lisa; or &reslisa;, it will reread its "
"configuration file (<userinput><command>killall</command> <option>-HUP lisa</"
"option></userinput>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:575
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you send the User1-Signal to &lisa; or &reslisa;, it will print some "
"status information to the standard output (<userinput><command>killall</"
"command> <option>-USR1 lisa</option></userinput>). You won't see anything if "
"the console from which &lisa;/&reslisa; was started has terminated."
msgstr ""

